2.1 Contributing documentation
==============================

The documentation here is written in Org mode.  The primary Info source
is ‘emacs-config.org’.  Each first-party module has its own Org page
‘docs/<feature>.org’, included from that master file.

   ‘my-docs.el’ provides the export helpers.  To keep the Info build
reproducible and independent of a personal Emacs session, export with
one of the two methods below.

   Do not run ‘M-x org-texinfo-export-to-info RET’ or
‘org-export-dispatch’ directly for the published manual.  Those paths
introduce noisy diffs from session-local settings.

* Menu:

* Interactively from Emacs::
* Using make::


File: emacs-config.info,  Node: Interactively from Emacs,  Next: Using make,  Up: Contributing documentation (1)

2.1.1 Interactively from Emacs
------------------------------

Requirements:
   • ‘emacs’ on ‘$PATH’

   Load the helper from the ‘docs/’ directory:

     (load (expand-file-name "my-docs.el"))

   Then run ‘M-x my-docs-export RET’.

   If you develop inside this checkout, set
‘my-docs-export-use-user-emacs-directory’ to ‘t’ so the exporter finds
the ‘docs/’ tree under ‘user-emacs-directory’.


File: emacs-config.info,  Node: Using make,  Prev: Interactively from Emacs,  Up: Contributing documentation (1)

2.1.2 Using ‘make’
------------------

Requirements:
   • ‘emacs’ on ‘$PATH’
   • ‘make’ on ‘$PATH’

   From the ‘docs/’ directory:

     make docs

   Running ‘make docs’ builds ‘emacs-config.info’ when the Org sources
are newer than the Info file.


File: emacs-config.info,  Node: Documenting a module,  Next: Guidelines,  Prev: Contributing documentation (1),  Up: Contributing documentation

2.2 Documenting a module
========================

Add an Org file named for the feature, for example
‘docs/defaults-config.org’.  Feature name equals the ‘provide’ symbol
and the filename stem.  Keep ‘docs/MAP.org’ in sync in the same change.

   When the page is ready for the Info manual, include it from
‘emacs-config.org’:

     #+include: defaults-config.org

   Export with ‘make docs’ (or ‘M-x my-docs-export RET’) and open a PR.


File: emacs-config.info,  Node: Guidelines,  Prev: Documenting a module,  Up: Contributing documentation

2.3 Guidelines
==============

   • Provide examples.
   • Use correct grammar and Australian English spelling in prose.
   • Spellcheck your work.
   • Use consistent Org markup:
     use ‘=’ for
             • file paths, e.g.  ‘init.el’
             • buffer names, e.g.  ‘*Messages*’
             • values (except numbers, which stay plain), e.g.  ‘nil’
             • keystrokes or keybindings, e.g.  ‘C-n’
             • text (to be) entered into the minibuffer (unless it is a
               complete command name), e.g.  ‘buf’
             • info nodes, e.g.  ‘(emacs)Just Spaces’
     use ‘~’ for
             • module names, e.g.  ‘defaults-config’
             • variable names, incl.  external ones, e.g.
               ‘load-prefer-newer’, ‘$PATH’
             • function names, e.g.  ‘car’
             • command names, incl.  external ones, e.g.  ‘find-file’,
               ‘make’
             • package names, incl.  ones that are shaped like a file
               name, e.g.  ‘denote’, ‘package.el’
   • Preview the Info document in Emacs to confirm the formatting.
   • Use ‘@@texinfo:@code{}@@’ or ‘@@texinfo:@samp{}@@’ where Org markup
     does not translate cleanly to Texinfo.
   • End sentences with two spaces in new Org prose that will be tangled
     or checked alongside Elisp style.


